# This file is symlinked to ~/.zshenv by software/comply365/zsh.rb.
#
# ============================================================================
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# ============================================================================
# We use Bash, not zsh. dotfiles configures Bash and otherwise never touches
# zsh — this is the single, deliberate exception.
#
# Some work repos ship git hooks written in zsh (e.g. web-safety's pre-commit /
# pre-push start with `#!/bin/zsh`). Git runs those hooks on every commit/push,
# including when you commit from a GUI app like Sourcetree.
#
# The problem: a GUI app is NOT launched from your terminal, so the zsh it
# spawns for a hook starts with a bare, minimal PATH — one that does NOT include
# Homebrew. Everything installed through Homebrew (pnpm, node, direnv, …) is
# then invisible, and the hook dies with "command not found" even though the
# tools are installed. (From a terminal it happens to work only because the hook
# inherits the terminal's already-populated PATH — a coincidence we don't want
# to rely on.)
#
# The fix: ~/.zshenv is the ONE startup file zsh reads on EVERY invocation —
# login, interactive, script, or a hook spawned by a GUI — before .zprofile and
# .zshrc. So it's the correct, future-proof place to guarantee the tools are
# findable no matter how a repo decides to run its zsh hooks.
#
# This affects zsh only. Your interactive Bash experience is completely
# unchanged. Since zsh reads this file on every invocation — including hooks
# nested inside a shell that already ran it — neither block below may cost
# anything or change anything the second time round: the Homebrew one is a no-op
# once its work is visible (see shellenv.sh), and nvm is only a stub until
# something actually calls it.
# ============================================================================

# ${(%):-%x} is this file (~/.zshenv) and `:A` resolves the symlink back to the
# dotfiles repo, so the snippets below are found wherever the clone lives.
# Deliberately not unset at the end: the lazy `nvm` stub below needs it when it
# is finally called, which may be long after this file has finished running.
__dotfiles_software="${${(%):-%x}:A:h}/.."

# --- Homebrew ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Put Homebrew — and everything installed through it (pnpm, node, direnv, git…)
# — on PATH. This is the crux of the whole file: without it a GUI-spawned zsh
# hook can't find any Homebrew tool. Same snippet interactive Bash uses.
\. "$__dotfiles_software/homebrew/shellenv.sh"

# --- nvm --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Make the `nvm` shell function available so a zsh hook can select a project's
# Node version from its .nvmrc. We load the SAME nvm that Bash uses (Homebrew's,
# via software/node) — not a second copy — through the shared loader.
#
# LAZILY, though. Sourcing nvm.sh costs ~200ms (it ends in `nvm_auto use`, which
# execs node), and unlike the Homebrew block above that cost is paid by *every*
# zsh this file is read by: `zsh -c` one-liners, and pre-commit runners that
# spawn one zsh per changed file. Almost none of them ever touch nvm.
#
# So define a stub that swaps itself for the real nvm on first use. Nothing is
# lost by waiting: our `nvm alias default system` means sourcing nvm.sh resolves
# `default` and settles on system Node — it does *not* auto-select the .nvmrc
# version — so eager loading only ever made the `nvm` name available, which is
# precisely what the stub does.
nvm() {
  unfunction nvm
  \. "$__dotfiles_software/node/nvm-loader.sh"
  nvm "$@"
}
